[20/200] Running: Compare data parallelism and pipeline parallelism....

🧠 LLM Response:


Data parallelism and pipeline parallelism are two different types of parallelism used in distributed computing to improve the performance of machine learning models.

Data parallelism involves splitting the training data into multiple chunks and distributing them across different worker nodes in a cluster. Each worker node processes a different chunk of data using the same model parameters. After each worker node completes processing its chunk of data, the model parameters are updated based on the gradients computed from each worker node's processing. This process is repeated for multiple iterations until the model converges or reaches a predetermined number of iterations.

Pipeline parallelism, on the other hand, involves splitting the model architecture into multiple stages and distributing them across different worker nodes in a cluster. Each worker node is responsible for processing a different stage of the model architecture. The output of each stage is sent as input to the next stage, creating a pipeline of processing. This enables the computation of each stage to be performed in parallel, improving the overall performance of the model.

In summary, data parallelism involves parallelizing the training data across different worker nodes, while pipeline parallelism involves parallelizing the model architecture across different worker nodes. Both techniques can be used together to further improve the performance of distributed machine learning models.
