**Answer**: The tokenizer's vocabulary is a set that contains all the tokens (words or subwords) that the tokenizer can recognize. When the tokenizer processes text, it breaks down the text into tokens from this vocabulary. For example, if a tokenizer's vocabulary contains the words "cat", "dog", and "mouse", then the tokenizer would break down the sentence "The cat chased the mouse but the dog did not." into the tokens ["The", "cat", "chased", "the", "mouse", "but", "the", "dog", "did", "not", "."]. The vocabulary is usually created during the training process, where the tokenizer learns the most common tokens from a large corpus of text data. The vocabulary size is usually a hyperparameter that is selected by the user. A larger vocabulary size typically leads to better recognition of rare words, but also increases the computational cost of the tokenizer.
[31/200] Running: What is causal masking in transformer decoders?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Causal masking in a transformer decoder is a mechanism used to ensure autoregressive property during generation. Autoregressive means that the output at each timestep is dependent only on the previous timesteps. During training, autoregressive models are trained to predict the next word given the previous words. During inference, autoregressive models generate one word at a time by conditioning on the previously generated words.

Causal masking is used to enforce this autoregressive property during inference in a transformer decoder. In a transformer decoder, self-attention is used to compute the attention weights between the input tokens at each timestep. Causal masking is applied to the attention weights to ensure that the attention weights at each timestep only depend on the previous timesteps.

The causal mask is a lower triangular matrix with ones on the diagonal and zeros above the diagonal. This matrix is multiplied element-wise with the attention weights to mask out the attention weights corresponding to future timesteps.

In other words, causal masking prevents the transformer decoder from attending to future timesteps during inference. This is important because if the transformer decoder were to attend to future timesteps during inference, it would be able to see the target sequence, which would violate the autoregressive property and result in overfitting.

In summary, causal masking is a mechanism used in transformer decoders to enforce autoregressive property during generation by ensuring that the attention weights at each timestep only depend on the previous timesteps. This is achieved by applying a lower triangular mask to the attention weights, which masks out the attention weights corresponding to future timesteps.
