[33/200] Running: Explain the difference between encoder-decoder and decoder-o...

🧠 LLM Response:


Encoder-decoder models consist of two components: an encoder and a decoder. The encoder takes in a sequence of input tokens and produces a sequence of hidden states, which capture the semantics and context of the input sequence. The decoder then takes these hidden states and generates the output sequence, token by token.

Decoder-only models, on the other hand, do not have an encoder component. Instead, they take in only the previous tokens in the output sequence and generate the next token in the sequence. Decoder-only models are typically used for tasks such as language modeling and text generation, where the goal is to generate a sequence of text that is coherent and grammatically correct, without needing to explicitly encode any input sequence.

In summary, encoder-decoder models are used when there is an input sequence that needs to be processed and encoded, and an output sequence that needs to be generated based on that input. Decoder-only models, on the other hand, are used when there is no explicit input sequence, and the goal is to generate a sequence of text that is coherent and meaningful on its own.
[34/200] Running: What is zero-shot vs few-shot vs fine-tuned inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 What are the main differences between these approaches and the advantages they bring along? In this blog post, let us consider these approaches and discuss how they differ in terms of their reliance on data and inference performance.

## Zero-shot inference

Consider a classification task that involves previously unseen classes at inference. Zero-shot inference refers to the ability to reliably predict one of the unseen classes given only their textual representations, such as one-hot encoded class labels or more informative, free-text natural language descriptions. Fig. 1 illustrates the zero-shot classification scenario.

Fig. 1. Zero-shot classification. To classify samples from previously unseen classes, zero-shot inference relies on the associations found in their name and/or description in the form of text embeddings. Zero-shot classification is usually possible thanks to the inter-class relationships known beforehand.

So, how does zero-shot inference accomplish this goal? By utilizing relations between classes that are learned during pretraining on naturally occurring text, such as——co-occurrences found on different scales. In simple terms, text embeddings memorize the latent associations^{1} between words that are “discoverable” in unlabelled textual data. such as word-level co-occurrences (). These learned inter-class relationships are also represented in textual form (as embeddings), which means that they correspond to something meaningful for humans. It is possible to transfer these associations to solve previously unforeseen problems at the inference stage by making use of the encoded class-specific information in the embeddings.

Class-specific information found in embeddings is extracted by different techniques depending on the model architectures. Some include using external attention mechanisms over precomputed class embeddings or by making use of explicit class representation heads that share network weights with the main model. Zero-shot learning algorithms mostly focus on associating name strings or natural language descriptions with the specific feed-forward properties associated with each class vector.

In practice, creating a good fixed feature extractor for zero-shot is crucial, because feeding the label embeddings to a separate feature extractor can improve the zero-shot capabilities but also reduce the ability for fine-tuning given a small number of labelled examples, which is exactly the opposite of the goal in few-shot learning settings (more on this later in this post).

In summary, with zero-shot inference, it is possible to predict previously unseen classes with very high accuracy, assuming access to their textual description during training. This allows for the annotations to be elicited relatively inexpensively.

## Few-shot inference

Fig. 2 shows what few-shot inference is about.

Fig. 2. Few-shot classification. Given a support dataset of 1- or few- labelled examples, few-shot classification aims at generalizing better to novel classes.

What makes few-shot classification different from zero-shot classification is that a few labelled examples from the _target_ classes are gonna be available at test time (and therefore may also be used during training). The idea is to leverage these labelled examples so that performance is improved over zero-shot learning when generalizing to novel classes. This is not possible with zero-shot learning, due to the absence of any labelled target class examples, hence such learn-ability is impossible.

The labelled examples from the target classes, called the support set, contain up to a few instances from each class. A typical setting is k$\times$ways, where k denotes the number of examples per class. The models are evaluated on a separate set of samples, called the query set. The idea is to observe the given support examples, and for the given query set samples, to predict their cross-entropy classification accuracy over all k classes assigned by the model.

To accomplish few-shot learning, a variety of learning techniques have been proposed in the literature. For example, with the memory-augmented neural network^{2}, the idea is to augment the main model with a key–value memory matrix (access and write “heads”) so that the memory access is a write-once, read-many system with access controlled by the main network. In^{3}, a simple non-parametric adaptive nearest-neighbor approximation of an LSTM using the embeddings learned during the pretrain stage, called prototypical network, is used. In^{4}, a support abstract vector is taken from a learned support net, whose weights are determined by a low-rank factorization over the observed support set.

