[44/200] Running: What is streaming inference and how is it used?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Streaming inference, also known as real-time or edge inference, enables inferencing applications to work locally on a device without an internet connection. By performing inferencing locally on a device, users can experience low-latency predictions, while companies can reduce the need to send sensitive information to external clouds, enhancing their privacy. These implementations can be used for a variety of applications, including visual search and computer vision.

However, while beneficial, real-time inference demands large amounts of processing power, which can be difficult to manage on resource-constrained devices. Implementing streaming inference requires a smaller model, which can perform fast enough to address the latency constraints of the application.

This is why model quantization is often used as a way to reduce a model’s size, while retaining accuracy. While quantization can reduce model size by as much as 8x, the process must be handled carefully to ensure minimum loss of information.

An implementation challenge is to achieve high efficiency while maintaining Deep Neural Network (DNN) performance, but this is where AWS Inferentia comes to the rescue. The AWS Inferentia chip combines massive amounts of high-quality compute, along with custom-designed hardware accelerators. The result is an efficient machine learning platform that enables accurate, low-latency predictions, encompassing a range of workloads.

Advantages of streaming inference

The ability to run inference locally on a device offers a wide range of advantages compared to typical cloud or data center-based implementations:

1. Enhanced performance
Running inference locally means that users can enjoy the benefits of real-time predictions. They’ll also be able to work offline and without interruptions, something which is difficult to achieve in cloud-based inference due to network latency.
2. Privacy and security
Edge device deployments means that sensitive information doesn’t need to be sent to a cloud or data center, ensuring user privacy and increasing security.
3. Economic efficiency
Edge device implementations, as well as model quantization, can reduce the costs incurred by the need for computing resources in cloud-based solutions.

How to use AWS Inferentia to implement streaming inference:

In order to implement real-time inference, you must start with a model that is small enough to fit onto the device. You can then quantize this model to an appropriate size, while maintaining network performance. Next, you can compile the model for inference on AWS Inferentia.

Amazon SageMaker provides several tools to facilitate the process of building low-latency, high-throughput streaming inference models for AWS Inferentia such as :

- Neo (Neural Engine Optimizer), a compiler that maps Deep Learning models onto AWS Inferentia.
- Support for Inference Open Neural Network Exchange (ONNX), a widely used interchange format for optimizing and deploying machine learning models.
- Compiler specific Amazon SageMaker models, including ResNet-50, R-CNN, AlexNet, and more.

To learn more about AWS Inferentia, check out the documentation on AWS.

Summary

Implementing streaming inference on edge devices offers a wide range of benefits, including enhanced performance, privacy, and cost efficiency. However, real-time inference demands fast models capable of performing high-quality inferencing on resource-constrained devices.

AWS Inferentia is a high-quality machine learning chip designed to offer real-time inference and large scale machine learning deployments for production workloads. The heavily customized hardware and high quality compute provide substantial advantages to implement accelerated machine learning on edge devices, paired with a user-friendly infrastructure to assist create minimum viable models with low latency and high accuracy.

While AWS Inferentia offers significant benefits, it is essential to maintain the highest possible performance while using small enough models to fit onto the devices. To learn more about real-time inference on edge devices and AWS Inferentia, visit https://aws.amazon.com/inferentia/.

