🧠 LLM Response:


"TTFT" is a system valuable to reduce the average response time of a client trying to connect to a proxied service. There can still be some latency even after the TTFT "timeout" has elapsed and a connection is made - depending on the target service itself or the underlying network.
While solving TTFT issues does not deal directly with user perceived latency it can be a good first step, as fixing it can improve average response time.

User perceived latency will still depend on latency from networks such as mobile, home networks, etc. as a whole.
TTFT focuses on that initial connection (request) latency.

What is "TTFT"?
TTFT is short for "Time To First Byte", it describes the time it takes from the moment a client initiates a connection to the moment the server responds with a reply (represented by the first byte sent in the response).

Presentation of TTFT on the neutron probe dashboard

The TTFT is shown in the "Connection Health" section of the Dashboard tab.

The neutron probe interface compares the TTFT of the requested service (host name) through a neutral virtual machine compared to the TTFT of the same request through the target proxied service.
When a request is made through a proxied service, the initial TTFB will be that of connecting to the proxy server, then the proxied service itself. This could be where the neutron probe observes increased latency comparing the TTFB response to that of a neutral virtual machine connecting to a target service directly.

The leftmost half of the chart showcases the TTFB latency on both a proxied service (blue) and a neutral virtual machine (orange)

- Connections of a green color on the left bar indicate the TTFB was lower through the neutral VM (individual connections).
- Connections of a gray color on the left bar indicate the TTFB was lower through the proxied service (individual connections).
- The percentage shown is the average difference in TTFB between left and right bars.

The chart on the right hand side showcases the percentage of TTFB increase when using a proxy, compared to neutral VM connections.

- A 0% difference would mean no difference in latency in connection to the target or proxied host.
- A 100% difference would mean twice the latency when using a proxy, compared to the neutral VM.
- A -100% difference would mean half the latency compared to the latency through the neutral VM.

Towards the bottom of the page, the average TTFB for both proxied service and neutral VM responses are displayed for each named host (represented on either left or right bars accordingly).
A sorting function is available above the host lists so they can be ordered based on their created columns.
