🧠 LLM Response:
 Intuitively speaking, we might think that, in the process of encoding, a transformer models english text and french text separately in their own representations, from which the decoder can then generate the translation.

However, in many multilingual applications, this is not how transformer models are used.

> In the transformer layer, all token representations across languages are jointly encoded.

This comes with advantages and disadvantages. It’s more space-efficient and makes the model learn semantic and syntactic similarities between languages faster. The downside is that polyglot tokenizations (where a token acquires different embeddings in different languages, as in wordpiece) are intermingled, which is bad for monolingual tasks.

In fairseq, we can train a multilingual model using the `--task multilingual_translation` flag.

# Modifier

A modifier layer modifies a given set of representations according to some property of the input, eg:

- language
- speaker
- position
- token type
- length of sequence

 Some modifiers are also called prompt functions (e.g in BERT)

## Using a modifier in a fairseq transformer

To add a modifiers given $n_m$ modifier groups (each modifier group containing $n_g$ different modifiers), we create a new function `add_multi_modifiers_to_embedding` that merges modifiers with the $e_1, e_2, ... e_k$ input embeddings and creates a new embedding of shape $(n_m\times n_g \times k), d$ (followed by a relu activation), obtaining a shape $(k, d )$ with one of the orchestrator sequence functions :

- depends_nd ()
- add (1D)
- add (2D)
- attend
- cat

We can also decide to add a linear ordering of the modifiers. I will look into that more later (see this post). There is no option of applying the feature-wise operation directly to the embeddings (which might be more natural), since thats only allowed within the transformer itself (not in the embeddings).
