🧠 LLM Response:

================================================

In this notebook, we will explore **metrics that are used to evaluate the performance of large language models** **trained on data from multiple languages, also called multilingual language models** (MLMs).

This notebook includes:
1. Presentation of a well-known example of multilingual language model: Mosaic.
2. Description of the main metrics used to evaluate their performances.
3. Results on several datasets!

## 1. Presentation of a multilingual language model

**mT5**: is the multilingual version of Facebook AI's [T5](https://ai.facebook.com/blog/multilingual-seq2seq-t5-mt5/): a Transformer model with a text-to-text objective, trained with sequence-to-sequence and span overlap objectives on 30 different languages. Thanks to its pretraining, mT5 performs a wide range of NLP tasks, such as Language Modelling, Named Entity Recognition (NER) or Machine Translation, learning internal priors which help it to better achieve these tasks.


**Why learning with such inner priors can help on these tasks?**

- After learning an internal, task-agnostic, task-conditioned modeling of language and form, mT5 has the ability to perform, after fine-tuning, fine-grained modeling like text classification.
- Fine-tuning with a limited amount of labeled data and using the priors learned during pre-training enables to use a high amount of unlabeled data and to achieve strong performance on a wide range of languages.

**Practical use**: Researchers often reuse an already trained mT5 (e.g. by fine-tuning a pre-trained model), but developers may fine-tune and serve mT5 on production tasks.

For more information, you can refer to <https://ai.facebook.com/blog/multilingual-seq2seq-t5-mt5/>.

## 2. Metrics to evaluate multilingual models

To measure the performance of mT5, diverse metrics (mathematical quantities), as [accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision) and [macro-f1 score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_score), can be used.

Let's define some of them:

- **Per sample metrics**: these metrics are used to evaluate task performance on a single example at a time.
    - **Accuracy**: For a single sample (for example a paragraph or a sentence), the accuracy is the proportion of words (tokens) that have been correctly predicted between the ground truth sentence and the one predicted by the model.
    - **Perplexity**: This measures how well the model predicts data into one probable set. Number of possible outcomes "guessed by" the model is the degree of unpredictability in the data.

- **Per dataset metric**
    - **Global Accuracy**: Accuracy over the whole dataset.
    - **Weighted F1 score**: Micro and macro F1 are two specific ways of calculating an F1 score. The macro F1 score gives a big picture of how the two classes are performing, whereas the weighted F1 accounts more for the distribution of the data.
    - **Average Perplexity**: Perplexity calculated on the whole estimation dataset, can be averaged to compare different models performance on this dataset.

Putting it in practice, let's calculate those metrics on the validation set of several datasets!


