🧠 LLM Response:


=====================================================

Goal
----

Neural networks seem to be good at fitting highly complex, nonlinear relationships. However, they struggle at generalizing from datasets that they have been trained on to other (similar) data.
The attention bottleneck entails loosing information by neglecting essential parts of the input due to the structural limits of neural networks.

The goal is to prevent the attention bottleneck by selecting the subset of input features at each step of the sequence, computing the forward and backward passes, and updating the subset of input features again, until all input has been considered. This subset sampling should be reapplied at the level of each feature dimension.

Implementation
--------------




```python

import torch
import torch.nn as nn

'''
torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_value_
The `clip_grad_value_` function forces all gradients of each tensor to be in the interval specified (eg. -1 to 1).
The gradients are absolutely clipped.
'''
torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_value_(model.parameters(), clip_value=1.)
output = model(x)

# We want some gradients
optimizer.zero_grad()
output.backward(torch.ones_like(output))


```

