In rotary (or learnable) embeddings, the concept is to make the embedding space learnable and rotation invariant, rather than keeping it fixed as in standard embeddings. This is achieved by defining the embedding as a set of learnable parameters and applying a rotation matrix on it. This rotation matrix can be learned as a group of Euler angles, which makes the embeddings rotation invariant.
This approach has been shown to be effective in improving downstream tasks that rely on embeddings, such as natural language processing and computer vision. The idea is that by allowing the embedding space to be learned and rotated, the model can capture richer and more flexible representations of the data, which can lead to improved performance.
