Perhaps a visual aide of Transformer model would be helpful, I am trying to explain it without any visualization/diagram/picture.

At the heart of the Transformer, we have the self-attention module. I will explain a case with 3 identical input tensors. Each of them is converted to Q,K,V format to be fed to self-attention function, as briefly said:

self-attention ($Q$: queries, $K$: keys, $V$: values) =

Softmax(Q.K / sqrt(dim(Q))) $\times V$.

We have two self-attention blocks. For clarity let's denote the Q, K, and V above as X. As a result for i=1 and 2, we would get:

\begin{cases}
Z_1 = self-attention(X) \\
Z_2 = self-attention(Z_1)
\end{cases}

So Z_1 is output of the first block and gets fed into the second block. It is easy to see that Z_1 has a bigger dimension than X in your graph, so when X is normalized to Q, K, and V, we get Z_1 in the first output.

Please note that dimension is [batch size, sequence length, embedding size] for X and Z_1, but $[batch size, sequence length, numberOfHeads \times embeddingSize]$ for Q,K,V and Z_2. All the Q,K,V have the same dimensions. Number of heads is 4 and embedding size is 64 in your graph.

