[96/200] Running: Explain the concept of auto-regressive generation....

🧠 LLM Response:

Auto-regressive generation is a type of generative model where the model generates a sequence of data points one at a time, with each data point being generated based on the previously generated data points. This process is repeated until a complete sequence is generated. In other words, the model generates data in an auto-regressive manner, using its own previous outputs to inform the current output.

For example, in the case of text generation, an auto-regressive model might generate a sentence one word at a time, with each word being generated based on the previously generated words. The model would start with a beginning-of-sequence token, generate the first word, then use that word as input to generate the second word, and so on, until it generates an end-of-sequence token.

Auto-regressive generation has several advantages over other types of generative models. It can generate realistic and coherent sequences of data points, since each data point is generated based on the context provided by the previous data points. It can also handle variable-length sequences, since the model can generate as many data points as needed. However, auto-regressive generation can be computationally expensive, since it requires the model to generate each data point one at a time.
