[108/200] Running: What are residual connections in transformers?...

🧠 LLM Response:


In a transformer, the residual connection, or shortcut connection, refers to the direct, unaltered connection from a given input to a hidden layer to its very next output. Each residual block in the Transformer is composed of a self-attention module, followed by a feed-forward network. The input is initially added to the output of the self-attention module through the residual connection. The output of the feed-forward network is then added to the intermediate output from the self-attention module and residual connection. This structure allows the original input from the sequence data to be continually available in future layers, enabling the network to retain all of the given information and learn more efficiently.

In the diagram of a transformer block above, the small arrow represents the residual connection. Both the input and output dimensions at each point need to match for the residual connection to work. This is done by utilizing a linear layer to match the input and output dimensions for both the self-attention and the feed-forward network. When these dimensionalities match, the residual connection facilitates the efficient learning of complex structures even in deep networks.

See the following links for more information:
A Comprehensive Guide to Deep Learning with Transformers | by Zaccha Liang | Towards Data Science
https://towardsdatascience.com/a-comprehensive-guide-to-deep-learning-with-transformers-f745f0bd54e8

How the Machine Learns | by Scott Alexander
