[112/200] Running: How do checkpoints improve fault tolerance?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 I read some sources and all of them say that the checkpoints help in fault tolerance, but no technical details are mentioned about their usage in recovery. How is a process recovered from a checkpoint.

Checkpoint recovery works quite simple. For sake of simplicity, I'll assume that you use symmetric multiprocessing (whereas the regular Unix system architecture is SMP or *AS), and that your operating system supports this directly in the kernel.

The checkpointing unwinds what the operating system built up during cpuset_create(). Each task individually checkpoints. The quickest way to restore the entire set of tasks is to write a script that calls 'taskset' (or 'cpuset') associated with the preset. Each process would find itself in a new, empty cpuset (and hence, when it forks, the child is in a new, empty cpuset, and when it wait()s or exec()s, the child is handled the same, whether it's inside the tight cpuset or outside.)

The recovery manager keeps a table of which cpuset ID went with which release point: for example, for a monthly release promotable at 21:00, it knows that it needs to restore from the cpuset associated with "2014-01-01 21:00:00 +01:00" or whatever. When recovery restores the last database transaction (mysql's "SET GLOBAL sql_log_bin=0" and then "FLUSH LOGSes") it can reply 'OK' to the master (leader server). The master then releases the newly promoted (recovered) server to pick up new jobs.

There are some problems with this model, because suppose the shared volatile disks grant too many slots for the amount of memory already reserved. (For example, suppose the amount of available slot space is so much larger than the amount of RAM already attached and configured on the boxes that there's no way it'd ever recover before exhausting the available disk for the terminating volume in question.) In this case, the checkpoints aren't going to be as trivial, and you can either...

a) kick the server out of affinity with the terminating volume. Run recovery. After recovery, accept that the configuration has changed a bit and load OFFLINE data from the extra archive server, and then reattach the terminating volume. (If the data actually matches the main copy well enough relative to quality controls and other parameters.) Or simply accept that a periodic complete recovery will occur.

b) dump the volume to a fresh newly booted box that is configured to preserve the disk label and LVM system. That box shouldn't touch the tablespace metadata. Then recovery can occur, then the newly booted box is either put aside or integrated in to the cluster somehow.

Of course, there's the alternative a+b. Dump the volume to a newly recovered-into-existence machine and then integrate.

Ultimately, it's a design decision based on how important your architecture is for your computing goals. Some kinds of fault-tolerant systems will automatically use multiple machines like this for the same work, while distributed data stores will use a decentralized, simple prioritized communication system that reduces traffic by Selecting Max Min ER plugin and identifies the last person who has the 'goodest' copy before raising a flag.

In the sense of how Distributed Data Stores do it, you can use RAID-系列的分布式存储解决方案（太分散了） or, for a more centralized and easier to understand design, you can use RAID-系列的集中式存储解决方案, for two different example architectures and their terminology and system components. Here's the terms for the RAID-系列的分布式存储解决方案:

> Error-correcting codes converts the unreliable shards of information into reliable *i*-stripes, through **xi** bits of error code that represent parity on different permutations of the bits repeated across all *n* of i's replicas. Changing 1 bit makes **xi** bits of error code incompatible with **xi** other bits. Unlike RAID-5, which relies on the Hamming Code when writing, RAID-6 needs two different types of codes when writing.

To translate: xi bits of error code are copied in the i'th slot where n is the architecture profile (2 slots for RAID-1, 5 slots for RAID-5, 6
