One of the tasks that Canine accomplished was multi-modal translation.
Sharing embeddings between two or more embeddings has been shown to
have tremendous benefits. How were the embeddings shared between data
modalities?

Canine projects images down to 1D token embeddings before its
Transformer decoder processes them. The Authors focus a lot on how the
encode-decode-like pretraining was a result of training the Transformer
decoder on inputs (pseudo-prefix tokens, images) and ground truth
outputs in various compositions. These compositions are the key pieces
to how embeddings might have been shared:

-   Outputs only
-   Inputs only
-   Pseudo-prefix tokens only
-   Partitioned inputs (images were tokenized together with prefix
    tokens during pre-training)\
    In the last case multiple embeddings are used together as inputs
    (e.g. images and prefix tokens) and they get to interact together.
    The embeddings for the tokens in this partitioned input are shared
    in this case, as they end up being run through the same Transformer
    layers.

We can see that for outputs, unless they overlap modes, there is no
sharing of embeddings. Canine is limited by this, which gives researchers
motivation to looking into more innovative ways to share models
(e.g. Vision Transformers).

Two ideas for ways embeddings could be shared, include:

-  	 Embeddings could be taken from a dictionary, and all references
to them are held in memory as a function of this vocabulary\
	 New embeddings are generated by sampling and transforming them
in either the input space (pre) or the output space (post)
-   Pose this as a multi-task problem:
	 Given a numerical embeddings space (not text, not image, etc.):
	 - for Images, encode them somehow to a set of vectors with a
	possible varying length
	 - for Audio, encode them somehow to a set of vectors with a
	possible varying length
	 - for Text encode them into a set of vectors
	Then, train a Transformer to use any of these embeddings to output
	a new embedding. This is in line with things such as
	`Multimodal Representations for Universal Speech Recognition
	<https://johnwmillr.github.io/projects/publications/2022-MMR-UNIST/mills-mmr-unist-icassp2022.pdf>`__,
	which was published near the time that Canine was published.

Canine authors clearly favored the Model Annealing training-style. I
hypothesize that the compositions mentioned above used the left-over network
to encode embeddings for whatever the composition called for that was not
already an input, meaning that there was a full decoder left behind used
to build up embeddings from previous layers. I believe this because we
see that their training scheme consisted of decoder-only training,
where they mashed left-over networks with learned dilations from larger
transformer models to create more and more spare embeddings. I hypothesize
that this was because the output embeddings is where there were very
obvious opportunities for sharing—they attempted to create all the
different embeddings during it, but through the model compression techniques
(e.g. Model Annealing) they figured out that it was much more complex
than just one fully connected layer.

