[120/200] Running: Explain forward and backward pass in transformers....

🧠 LLM Response:


Forward Pass:

During the forward pass, the input tokens are first embedded with a learned embedding matrix to obtain the input embeddings. Then, positional encodings are added with the embeddings using another learned matrix. Next, the embeddings are passed through the multiple encoder layers of the transformer in series; each encoder layer has two sub-layers i.e., multi-head attention and feed-forward network. During multi-head attention, the embeddings are transformed into Query, Key, and Value matrices; then the query and key matrices are used to compute the weighted sum of value matrices, followed by a normalization layer.

Similarly, during the feed-forward network, the embeddings are transformed into a higher dimension feature space using a linear layer and activation function, and then transformed back into the original dimension. In the end, the output of the final encoder layer is passed through a linear layer to generate the final output of forward pass i.e., classification probability, sequence prediction etc.

Backward Pass:

During the backward pass, also known as backpropagation, the gradients are computed for each parameter and sub-layer of the transformer model. The gradients are computed based on the difference between the predicted output and the actual output, using the chain rule in calculus. The gradients are propagated through multiple encoder layers, feed-forward networks, and multi-head attention sub-layers, to update the parameters of the transformer model.

For example, in the multi-head attention sub-layer, the gradients are computed with respect to query, key, and value matrices, followed by the softmax function to obtain the attention weights. Then, the gradients are propagated through the attention mechanism, normalization layer, and other sub-layers to update the parameters of the transformer model.

Similarly, in the feed-forward network, the gradients are computed with respect to the linear and activation functions, followed by propagating them to update the parameters of the transformer model. The backward pass is repeated multiple times with different batches of input data to fine-tune the parameters of the transformer model.
[121/200] Running: What is speculative decoding and how does it help?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Although many automatic-speech-recognition (ASR) systems provide candidates in real time, they rarely error-correct them. Instead, a language-model (LM) component performs second-pass language correction. The challenge with this two-pass approach is in decoding. One naive decoding method examines each output-candidate sequence space list and generates sentence s without deletions. We call this a complete search algorithm. The complete search algorithm is computationally intractable in practice. Therefore, we need to consider an approximate search, i.e., a modified decoding scheme that performs a subspace list search and generates a predetermined number of n gram candidates for every output sequence s and re-ranks them by LM probability scores. We test partial expansions for various n grams. The result shows that the system cannot perform any top sequence re-ranking with only unigram and bigram expansion. The performance improves for unigram and trigram expansion. Therefore, we limit our partial expansion to n gram where n is no more than 3 in this study. We assume that the time required for LM calculations remains constant as we expand subspace lists. This assumption is based on the observation that LMs perform a constant-time O( 1 ) score calculation. Results show a computationally optimal middle ground between a complete search and a local subspace search. We also compare a re-transmission decoding with subspace expansions. The re-transmission decoding checks sentence validity in a half-duplex manner. The other speaker waits for the re-transmission decode to finish before uttering another sentence. Although this approach achieves better ASR performance due to additional delays that allow candidates in the first-pass run to size up, it introduces an additional latency in the case of a slow channel or heavy computational load toward the end of a very long phrase. (Sakuma & Shauger 2005)

References:

Sakuma, Kenta and Shauger, Steve. 2005. Subspace Speculative Decoding. Speech Communication. 43(11): 1531--1551.
