[150/200] Running: How does deep caching work in inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Deep cache acceleration is used to speedup matrix multiplication. The idea is to use high bandwidth “vector class” SRAM caches for low precision caching of matrix elements. The deep cache is realized by stacking these vector class caches on-chip and interfacing it with the compute units.

When performing deep learning computations, we employ matrix multiplication as the basic compute kernel. Instead of employing the conventional dense matrix multiplication kernels that offer poor reuse and low energy efficiency, we use a reordered data structure called block-sparse matrices. And instead of processing complete rows of a matrix (thus aggressively reshuffling data for caching), we use circular sliding windows to perform computations.

These block-sparse matrices, are computed in a tile format, in which storage and computations are organized in four distinct regions – product matrix B, the input-matrix A, the control signals MC and LUTT through which computations are performed on B and A to produce the output C.

During matrix multiplication, we require to load the matrices B, A and the control signals MC, LUTT from the primary memory into the deep SRAM cache. Thus, we divide these matrices into fixed-size tiles that best match the SRAM cache size. For caching the input matrix A, we organize them in a first-in-first-out (FIFO) structure, and for B, we arrange them along a circular caching structure as shown below. The index loading mechanism is shown in the Control plane. We use circular indices I_A, I_B that point to the data in the cache to perform matrix multiplication (more on this later). MC and LUTT contain metadata and/or computed list of operations corresponding to the cache slot indices I_A, I_B.

Deep SRAM caches before sidestep memory

Control Region

As shown in the control plane, we use different circuits to process the matrix B and the input-matrix A. For matrix-B, we use the GeNN library, that enables optimized mapping of the deep SRAM configurations for increasing data reuse. The index loading mechanism for controlling the data in the deep SRAM cache is also shown in this figure. The downside of this mechanism is that it does not use reuse-aware addressing.

Index loading mechanism. Data enter and exit the cache consecutively (left), occupying distinct slots. The index loader determines the cache slot index for a particular input d datum. This is translated to the index in the product matrix B.

More specifically, since these circular SRAM caches are abstract (not custom like the tiling kernels from Imagination), we can initialize the input indexes with specific values to help load data in a desired pattern. Convolutional kernel shape referencing the data in the circular B cache helps to realize optimized tiling of the input matrix and thus be optimized for fetching input data from memory. As previously mentioned, this does not enable reuse-aware addressing.

SRAM Input Cache

The control signals MC and LUTT are processed in a ring buffer Data Queue (DQ). The MC queue partitions each matrix-B tile into four fixed-size tiles that best match the block size. Thus, we always know that matrix-A data (spread over these four tiles) are always a distance 2\*block size away from where the matrix-B data is being accessed (as shown in the figure below). The state machine, start and end bits and the timer deliver control signals for writing or reading the input elements and thus steer computations. The retrieved values are then sent to the SRAM I/O for matrix multiplication.

Processing in the SRAM Input Cache (I_n)

Output region in SRAM Cache

The Matrix C and T4W (to-be-read four-way) buffers are used in the output region. The Matrix C buffer is used to store the resultants of matrix- multiplication, and its entries are sent to processing element. This occurrence map delivers  the start-bit, end-bit and cycle profiles to control computations. The start-bit and end-bit trigger and terminate input insertions into the SRAM cache during specific cycles, while the cycle-profile carries pre-configured cycles that determine the beginning or end of the processing window in the SRAM cache. The buffer T4W (or, to-be-read four-way) stores the MT and the PTT control signals. MT identifies four SRAM cache accesses for which PTT determines the weight distribution, which is then added to the output cached buffers.

[151/200] Running: What is a prompt-truncation error?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 \nA prompt-truncation error happens when the message that you send through the API is larger than 4096 B. In case of a prompt-truncation error, the entire prompt is cut off after the 4096 B threshold, and only the first 4096 B are sent to the model. This lack of input might negatively affect the output quality and change the overall meaning of your prompt.
