Warmup time is used to measure the time it takes for a model to reach threshold accuracy when a pipeline is started. It's used as a check for making sure that a model has warmed up to a performant state before generating predictions.

When training the model, the model during training warms up gradually, and this carries over to a new inference pipeline. During inference pipeline creation, a new instance of the model is usually created and warmed up. The amount of time to warm up can depend on several factors: The size of the model and of the input, the number of layers, the layer types, and the options passed to the `initialize` function. With respect to inference runtimes, warmup time can be considered as part of the startup cost.

