[170/200] Running: How does the tokenizer handle emojis and symbols?...

🧠 LLM Response:


<!--TASK: emojis + symbols-->

The tokenizer recognizes the symbols and emojis as unicode character sequences and extracts them as single units. An emoji in a social media post containing several words is still recognized as a single unit due to varying length of emoji sequences.

For an illustrative example, consider the following Twitter post:

![Twitter post frame with blue hover over text and emojis](images/emoji-tweet.jpg)

This text contains a variety of limited-length unicode characters, which span different symbol domains, as exemplified by the heart emojis (`❤️`, `💕`, `💔`). Using a bag-of-words token representation, the full content of the 280 character Tweet would be tokenized into the following 11 units:

```
['💥', 'People', 'love', '🔮', 'crystal', '💓', 'energy', 'so', 'much', '💜', '💖']
```

In this case, the user's expressed sentiment towards receiving crystals via the mail is spread across these 11 tokens despite containing two different emoticons 💥 and 💜, 🔮 and the word "love". 

This uneven distribution across the tokens hinders the derivation of a single sentiment score for predicting "positive", "neutral", or "negative" feelings towards the object of discussion. As a result, the preprocessing stage may introduce complications to downstream tasks that expect a higher semantic coherence and well-balanced token distribution.

To mitigate this issue, there are several possible approaches, which include emoji and reaction embedding and using hand-crafted labels as additional features. In the context oftransfer learning, emoji and reaction embeddings have to be learned and added to the scaling models. In either case, continuous updates in the on-line conversations depending heavily on culture lead to discrepancies in the modeling stage, as well as in the extraction of features.
[171/200] Running: How does model size affect inference latency?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Analyzing latency vs. model size helps software developers understand which model best fits the use case and hardware. As a thing may have many parts and may be composed of many things, model size can refer to total number of parameters, sum of 3D kernels, text file size or any number of related axes. This assessment compares just the number of model parameters, which is commonly available and correlated to other size metrics, as it captures the general shape of the curve.

This assessment looks at the mean value of the 99th percentile, the lowest 1% of inference latency results. From these results, we can pull out key insights on how well each model runs on the hardware being tested.

Results are summarized in Reference tables for inference latency vs. model size across devices and categories. This reference can help for:

- Pre-training for transfer learning: If your model size ends up similar to source models below, consider their performance.
- Fine-tuning for distillation: If your model size resembles the source model and is smaller than the budding student, generation will likely be faster on the same hardware.
- Evaluation of new hardware: Model size vs. performance metrics help understand emerging memory and compute frontiers.

Consistent with guidelines for model quality, the 99th percentile of latency is what we use to account for performance outliers. The average latency is not for production use, so it’s not included in the results. All sets of metrics capture a snapshot in time across similar devices.

This assessment provides results across a wide range of mobile, edge and cloud devices from Arm, Intel and NVIDIA as well as categories including tablets, smartphones, smart cameras and servers. Cross-device comparisons are rated against optimized inference engines and profiled alongside unoptimized TensorFlow Lite models when possible. This unifies cross-platform assessments by noting latency margins of the optimized software against base TensorFlow Lite.

You can also explore the results yourself across the devices in the results tables:

- Confirm: Model quality, inference latency, memory footprint, power use and other generalized aspects of benchmarks help frame the ecosystem of variables around machine learning. Check other common latency scenarios in this assessment for better reference of your actual use case.
- Collective gains: On-device ML solutions can call multiple models at different times and diverse models on the same input frames, so consider batching models on a per-frame basis in order to improve latency.
- Compute and memory frontiers: Explore this assessment over time and see how many new instruction set improvements and architectures can enhance latency.
- Custom-TFLite models: Consider the annotated base reference for the current software and experiment with your own models on the chosen hardware, looking for an optimal interaction. Keep attention on model size, to the number of parameters per tensor to keep TLite models in check.
- Mitigate outliers: If outliers manifest, they’re likely caused by memory contention, competition for memory bandwidth from other running activities on the device, or other direct interactions that don’t fit the current case set.

About the data used in this assessment:

See the reference tables for a full list of evaluated models. Note that more than just these aforementioned models are assessed in the assessment, but only the following represented single-image classification models are emphasized in this write-up:

- ResNet-10, 26, 32, 44, 50, 56, 101, 110, 152, 182 (by depth)
