[180/200] Running: How to measure and reduce TTFT on Gaudi?...

🧠 LLM Response:


User specified TTFT (Time To First Tile) corresponds to the number of in Flight operations (I/Os) for all views in an Event Selection for finding the worst case in Event View when processing that selection. This number also corresponds to the maximum number of output tasks (including Zombie and no-handler output tasks which are not visible) for that Event Selection, assuming that each task consists of just one view. (Versions Agents should use output mapping to find views, so can have multiple views in a single output task.)

You can estimate the TTFT from the number of input tasks, and your knowledge of what the output tasks will be. The exact number can be determined by analyzing the output mapping.

TTFT will be reduced by reducing the number of I/Os or increasing the throughput. Increasing throughput is good for many other reasons, so is a worthwhile thing to do. Reducing the number of I/Os is more work. The reason for this is that the Gaudi scheduler is pretty much set to maximize the number of I/Os, since they are generally the bottleneck.

For example, assuming you have huge views. If these views are collected into super-views of just less than maximum view size, to reduce the number of I/Os, then the number of jobs done will double.

It is likely that less-than-a-billion-event Job-Throughput (JT) will not be reachable by any method, and you will need to consider changing business model (e.g. by breaking the large selection into smaller ones handled by different PHA) or to go outside of changes to the framework. This applies especially when your ESECTs have low JT.
